var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"S. Bandyopadhyay, S. K. Pal and B. Aruna. Multiobjective GAs, Quantitative Indices, and Pattern Classification. IEEE Transactions on Systems, Man, and Cybernetics, Part B (Cybernetics) 34, 2088–2099 (2004).\n\n\n\nA. L. Custódio, J. F. Madeira, A. I. Vaz and L. N. Vicente. Direct Multisearch for Multiobjective Optimization. SIAM Journal on Optimization 21, 1109–1140 (2011).\n\n\n\nK. Deb, A. Pratap, S. Agarwal and T. Meyarivan. A fast and elitist multiobjective genetic algorithm: NSGA-II. IEEE Transactions on Evolutionary Computation 6, 182–197 (2002).\n\n\n\nE. Zitzler and L. Thiele. Multiobjective optimization using evolutionary algorithms — A comparative case study. In: Parallel Problem Solving from Nature — PPSN V, Lecture Notes in Computer Science (Springer, 1998); pp. 292–301.\n\n\n\nE. Zitzler. Evolutionary Algorithms for Multiobjective Optimization: Methods and Applications. Ph.D. Thesis, ETH Zürich (1999).\n\n\n\nC. M. Fonseca, L. Paquete and M. López-Ibáñez. An Improved Dimension-Sweep Algorithm for the Hypervolume Indicator. In: 2006 IEEE International Conference on Evolutionary Computation (2006); pp. 1157–1163.\n\n\n\nC. Audet, J. Bigeon, D. Cartier, S. Le Digabel and L. Salomon. Performance indicators in multiobjective optimization. European Journal of Operational Research 292, 397–422 (2021).\n\n\n\nN. Fazzio, L. F. Prudente and M. L. Schuverdt. An active-set method for box-constrained multiobjective optimization. Optimization Online preprint (2025).\n\n\n\nH. Ishibuchi, R. Imada, Y. Setoguchi and Y. Nojima. How to Specify a Reference Point in Hypervolume Calculation for Fair Performance Comparison. Evolutionary Computation 26, 411–440 (2018).\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"metrics/hypervolume/#Hypervolume","page":"Hypervolume","text":"The hypervolume of a set F with respect to a reference point r is the volume of the region dominated by F and bounded by r [4], [5] (Def. 8), [7]:\n\nmathrmHV(F r) = lambda_mBigl(bigcup_y in F y_1 r_1 times dots times y_m r_mBigr)\n\nHigher is better. hypervolume computes it exactly:\n\nm = 2: sort by the first objective and add one rectangle per point;\nm = 3: the dimension sweep of Paquete et al. described in [6] (Section III-A), sweeping the third objective and keeping the non-dominated projections on the first two;\nm ge 4: the slicing recursion of [6] (Algorithm 1) along the last objective, down to the three-dimensional case.\n\nThe value depends strongly on r, so the reference point is always an argument [9].","title":"Hypervolume"},{"category":"section","location":"metrics/hypervolume/#Normalized-hypervolume","page":"Hypervolume","text":"normalized_hypervolume follows the convention of [8] (Section 7.2): map each objective by tilde y_j = (y_j - mathrmideal_j)(mathrmnadir_j - mathrmideal_j) with ideal and nadir of the reference front, take r = (11 dots 11), and divide by 11^m so that the value lies in 0 1:\n\nmathrmHV_mathrmnorm(F) = fracmathrmHV(tilde F (11 dots 11))11^m\n\nNormalizing and placing the reference point slightly beyond the nadir is common practice, but no numerical rule is standard: [9] shows that 11 is not always appropriate and proposes a rule that depends on the number of points and objectives. The offset 11 and the division by 11^m are therefore a convention, adjustable through offset.\n\njulia> using MultiObjectiveMetrics\n\njulia> hypervolume([[1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 1.0]], [3.0, 3.0])\n3.0\n\njulia> R = [[0.0, 1.0], [1.0, 0.0]];\n\njulia> normalized_hypervolume(R, R) ≈ 0.21 / 1.21\ntrue","title":"Normalized hypervolume"},{"category":"function","location":"metrics/hypervolume/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.hypervolume","page":"Hypervolume","text":"hypervolume(F, reference)\n\nExact hypervolume of the point set F with respect to reference [4], [5]: the Lebesgue measure of the region dominated by F and bounded by reference,\n\nHV(F; r) = λ_m(∪_{y ∈ F} [y, r]).\n\nHigher is better. The reference point is always explicit: there is no default, because the value depends strongly on it [9]. Points that are not strictly better than reference in every objective contribute nothing; dominated and repeated points do not change the value. An empty set, or a set with no contributing point, has hypervolume zero.\n\nAlgorithms: sorting for m = 2; the dimension sweep of Paquete et al. described in [6] (Section III-A) for m = 3, in O(n²) worst-case time with a sorted vector instead of a balanced tree; and the slicing recursion of [6] (Algorithm 1), down to the three-dimensional case, for m ≥ 4.\n\nSee normalized_hypervolume for the normalized variant.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> hypervolume([[1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 1.0]], [3.0, 3.0])\n3.0\n\njulia> hypervolume([[1.0, 1.0, 1.0]], [2.0, 3.0, 4.0])\n6.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.hypervolume"},{"category":"function","location":"metrics/hypervolume/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.normalize_points","page":"Hypervolume","text":"normalize_points(F, ideal, nadir) -> Vector{Vector}\n\nMap every point f of F to (f - ideal) ./ max.(nadir - ideal, eps), so that ideal goes to the origin and nadir to the vector of ones. The eps of the floating-point type guards objectives with ideal[j] == nadir[j].\n\nExamples\n\njulia> normalize_points([[1.0, 4.0], [3.0, 2.0]], [1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0])\n2-element Vector{Vector{Float64}}:\n [0.0, 1.0]\n [1.0, 0.0]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.normalize_points"},{"category":"function","location":"metrics/hypervolume/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.normalized_hypervolume","page":"Hypervolume","text":"normalized_hypervolume(F, R; offset = 1.1, tol = Tolerance())\nnormalized_hypervolume(F, ideal, nadir; offset = 1.1, tol = Tolerance())\n\nHypervolume of F after normalize_points, with reference point offset * ones(m), divided by offset^m:\n\nHV_norm(F) = HV(normalize(F); (offset, …, offset)) / offset^m.\n\nWith a reference front R, ideal = ideal_point(R) and nadir = nadir_point(R). The value lies in [0, 1]; higher is better, and for performance profiles use 1 / HV_norm.\n\nThis is the convention of [8] (Section 7.2), not a definition from the hypervolume literature: normalizing by ideal and nadir and taking a reference point slightly worse than the nadir is common practice, but offset = 1.1 and the division by offset^m are the authors' choices; see [9] for the effect of the reference point. Use hypervolume for any other reference point.\n\nReturns NaN when F is empty or when ideal ≈ nadir in every objective (under tol).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> R = [[0.0, 1.0], [1.0, 0.0]];\n\njulia> normalized_hypervolume(R, R) ≈ 0.21 / 1.21\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.normalized_hypervolume"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Installation","page":"Installation","text":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.jl requires Julia 1.10 or later and has no runtime dependencies. It is not registered yet; add it from its GitHub repository:\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/VectorOptimizationGroup/MultiObjectiveMetrics.jl\")\n\nTo work on a local checkout, develop it into your environment instead:\n\nPkg.develop(path = \"/path/to/MultiObjectiveMetrics.jl\")\n\nRun the tests from the package directory:\n\njulia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/profiles/#Performance-profiles","page":"Performance profiles","text":"Purity, Γ, Δ and the normalized hypervolume compare fronts obtained by several solvers on the same problem. To summarize a test set, the usual practice is a performance profile [2]: for each problem p and solver s, a cost t_ps where lower is better.\n\nMetric Cost t_ps\nPurity 1 / purity(A, R) (Inf when no point of A is in R)\nΓ-spread gamma_spread(A, R)\nΔ-spread delta_spread(A, R)\nNormalized hypervolume 1 / normalized_hypervolume(A, R)\n\nFor each problem, build R from the non-dominated fronts of all solvers compared (and of all runs, if available), so that the extremes of Γ and Δ and the membership test of Purity are the same for every solver [2]:\n\nusing MultiObjectiveMetrics\n\n# fronts[p][s]: objective vectors returned by solver s on problem p\nT = fill(NaN, nproblems, nsolvers)\nfor p in 1:nproblems\n    nd = [nondominated(fronts[p][s]) for s in 1:nsolvers]\n    R = nondominated(reduce(vcat, nd))\n    for s in 1:nsolvers\n        T[p, s] = gamma_spread(nd[s], R)\n    end\nend\n\nT can be passed to a profile package such as BenchmarkProfiles.jl, which treats NaN, Inf and negative entries as failures. The Conventions of this package return NaN or Inf exactly in the cases where a metric carries no information (empty front, single point, degenerate reference), so those cases count as failures, and never return negative values.\n\nPurity depends on which solvers enter R: two similar solvers share the reference points and both lose against a third one. [2] therefore compares Purity in pairs of solvers.","title":"Performance profiles"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/conventions/#Conventions","page":"Conventions","text":"The sources define the metrics for non-empty fronts in general position and do not discuss tolerances or degenerate cases. Where they are silent, this package follows the conventions of MetricsRel.jl from MAMBO-paper [8], except in the two cases marked below, where a literal reading of the source gives a defined value.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/conventions/#Edge-cases","page":"Conventions","text":"Case Γ, Δ Purity Covering Normalized HV Reason\nempty front A (or B for covering) NaN NaN NaN NaN no information; a failure in profiles\none point in A Inf defined defined defined Δ needs at least two points (the mean gap is over N-1 gaps); Γ follows Δ\nideal[j] ≈ nadir[j] objective j skipped — — guarded by eps a constant objective has no spread\nideal ≈ nadir in every objective NaN — — NaN no range to measure\npoints of A outside [ideal, nadir] clipped — — contribute zero if beyond the reference point see below\nall points of A equal in objective j Δ: Δ_j = 1 — — — see below\n\nEquivalence (≈) is the tolerance of Tolerance. For Purity and Covering, equivalent points count once, and Covering first reduces both sets to their non-dominated points (the source assumes non-dominated sets). For Γ and Δ the front is used as given, so repeated points lower the spread score, as clustering should.","title":"Edge cases"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/conventions/#Points-beyond-the-extremes-(Γ-and-Δ)","page":"Conventions","text":"Γ and Δ measure the front of one solver between extreme values taken from the reference front R, the non-dominated points of all solvers [2]. Formulas (5.1) and (5.2) of that paper sort the values of each objective with the extremes at both ends, so they assume that the front lies between the extremes. A point of A that R dominates can violate this: its value may exceed the nadir of R.\n\nUsed literally, the last gap δ_Nj = mathrmnadir_j - max_i f_ij becomes negative and is subtracted from the numerator of Δ. A front made entirely of dominated points can then score Δ = 0, the best possible value, or a negative Δ. Example: with R = [[0, 1], [0.5, 0.5], [1, 0]], the front A = [[0.6, 1.2], [1.2, 0.6], [3.0, 0.1]] (all three points dominated by R) gets Δ = 0 with the literal formula, the same value as R itself.\n\nThis package clips each value of A to mathrmideal_j mathrmnadir_j before computing Γ and Δ. Inside the box nothing changes; outside it, a point beyond the nadir is treated as sitting on the nadir. Then Δ ≥ 0 and Γ le max_j (mathrmnadir_j - mathrmideal_j) always hold. In the example, Δ(A) = 1.\n\njulia> using MultiObjectiveMetrics\n\njulia> R = [[0.0, 1.0], [0.5, 0.5], [1.0, 0.0]];\n\njulia> A = [[0.6, 1.2], [1.2, 0.6], [3.0, 0.1]];\n\njulia> delta_spread(A, R), delta_spread(R, R)\n(1.0, 0.0)","title":"Points beyond the extremes (Γ and Δ)"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/conventions/#Collapsed-objective-(Δ)","page":"Conventions","text":"If all points of A have the same value in objective j, every inner gap is zero and formula (5.2) gives Δ_j = 1: the numerator and the denominator both equal mathrmnadir_j - mathrmideal_j. This agrees with [3], where Δ equals one when all solutions coincide. The package uses this value. MetricsRel.jl skips such an objective instead.","title":"Collapsed objective (Δ)"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/conventions/#Differences-from-other-implementations","page":"Conventions","text":"Topic This package MetricsRel.jl (MAMBO)\nCovering weak dominance by default, as in [5] (Def. 9); StrictDominance() available strict dominance\nΓ, Δ outside [ideal, nadir] clipped literal formula (Δ can be negative)\nΔ with a collapsed objective Δ_j = 1 objective skipped\nNon-dominated filter with tolerance a point is kept when no other point dominates it sequential archive\nHypervolume exact, own implementation Metaheuristics.jl\n\nThe covering of Metaheuristics.jl also counts strict dominance, without tolerance.\n\nThe test suite checks every metric against values computed with MetricsRel.jl and asserts each of these differences explicitly. The parity cases include fronts inside and outside the box, duplicates, a single point, degenerate and collapsed objectives, and two to four objectives. With relation = StrictDominance() and with the front clipped, the values agree to rounding.","title":"Differences from other implementations"},{"category":"section","location":"metrics/purity/#Purity","page":"Purity","text":"Let mathcalF_s be the non-dominated front returned by solver s and mathcalF the non-dominated points of bigcup_s mathcalF_s. Purity is the fraction of the points of mathcalF_s that remain non-dominated when all fronts are merged [1] (eq. 7–8), [2] (Section 5.3.2):\n\nbar t_s = fracmathcalF_s cap mathcalFmathcalF_s in 0 1\n\nThe denominator is the size of the solver's own front. Higher is better; in a performance profile use t_s = 1bar t_s, which is +infty when the solver contributes no point to mathcalF.\n\nIn purity, membership in mathcalF means equivalence to some point of R under the tolerance, and equivalent points of A count once, since the source treats fronts as sets.\n\njulia> using MultiObjectiveMetrics\n\njulia> A = [[0.0, 1.0], [0.6, 0.6], [1.0, 0.0]]; B = [[0.5, 0.5]];\n\njulia> R = nondominated(vcat(A, B));\n\njulia> purity(A, R), purity(B, R)\n(0.6666666666666666, 1.0)","title":"Purity"},{"category":"function","location":"metrics/purity/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.purity","page":"Purity","text":"purity(A, R; tol = Tolerance())\n\nPurity of the front A of one solver with respect to the reference front R [1], [2]:\n\npurity(A, R) = |A ∩ R| / |A|\n\nR is normally the non-dominated set of the union of the fronts of all solvers compared, nondominated(reduce(vcat, fronts)). A point of A belongs to R when it is equivalent to some point of R under tol. Equivalent points of A count once (see unique_points); otherwise A is used as given, so pass the non-dominated front of the solver. The result lies in [0, 1], higher is better; for performance profiles use 1 / purity.\n\nReturns NaN when A is empty.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> A = [[0.0, 1.0], [0.6, 0.6]]; B = [[0.5, 0.5], [1.0, 0.0]];\n\njulia> R = nondominated(vcat(A, B));\n\njulia> purity(A, R), purity(B, R)\n(0.5, 1.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.purity"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"The indicators are documented on their pages: purity, gamma_spread, delta_spread, covering, hypervolume, normalize_points and normalized_hypervolume.","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Package","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Package"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Tolerance-and-dominance","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Tolerance and dominance"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Point-sets","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Point sets"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics","page":"API Reference","text":"Performance indicators for multiobjective optimization: dominance with tolerance, non-dominated filtering, Purity, Γ-spread, Δ-spread, Covering and exact hypervolume. All objectives are minimized.\n\nA point set is any AbstractVector of objective vectors, for example a Vector{Vector{Float64}}, eachcol(M) or eachrow(M). See purity, gamma_spread, delta_spread, covering and hypervolume.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.Tolerance","page":"API Reference","text":"Tolerance(atol, rtol)\nTolerance(tol)\nTolerance()\n\nComponentwise tolerance used to compare objective values. For components a and b, the tolerance is\n\nε = max(atol, rtol * max(|a|, |b|))\n\nTolerance(tol) sets atol = rtol = tol, which gives ε = tol * max(1, |a|, |b|), the rule of [8]. Tolerance() uses tol = √eps(Float64) ≈ 1.49e-8, and Tolerance(0) compares exactly. Both values must be finite and nonnegative.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> isequivalent([1.0, 2.0], [1.0 + 1e-10, 2.0])\ntrue\n\njulia> isequivalent([1.0, 2.0], [1.0 + 1e-10, 2.0]; tol = Tolerance(0))\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.Tolerance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.DominanceRelation","page":"API Reference","text":"DominanceRelation\n\nResult of compare(u, v) for minimization: Dominates (u dominates v), DominatedBy (v dominates u), Equivalent (every component within the tolerance) or Incomparable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.DominanceRelation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.compare","page":"API Reference","text":"compare(u, v; tol = Tolerance()) -> DominanceRelation\n\nCompare objective vectors u and v (minimization) under the componentwise tolerance ε_i of Tolerance:\n\nDominates: u_i ≤ v_i + ε_i for all i and u_j < v_j - ε_j for some j;\nDominatedBy: the same with u and v swapped;\nEquivalent: |u_i - v_i| ≤ ε_i for all i;\nIncomparable: otherwise.\n\nThis is the rule of [8]; Tolerance(0) gives Pareto dominance.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> compare([1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 3.0])\nDominates::DominanceRelation = 1\n\njulia> compare([1.0, 3.0], [2.0, 2.0])\nIncomparable::DominanceRelation = 3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.compare"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.dominates","page":"API Reference","text":"dominates(u, v; tol = Tolerance()) -> Bool\ndominates(relation::AbstractDominance, u, v; tol = Tolerance()) -> Bool\n\nWhether u dominates v (minimization). Without relation, or with StrictDominance, this is compare(u, v; tol) == Dominates. With WeakDominance it is weakly_dominates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.dominates"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.weakly_dominates","page":"API Reference","text":"weakly_dominates(u, v; tol = Tolerance()) -> Bool\n\nWhether u weakly dominates v: u_i ≤ v_i + ε_i for every component, that is, u dominates v or is equivalent to it. This is the \"covers\" relation of [4] and [5] (Def. 4).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> weakly_dominates([1.0, 2.0], [1.0, 2.0])\ntrue\n\njulia> dominates([1.0, 2.0], [1.0, 2.0])\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.weakly_dominates"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.isequivalent","page":"API Reference","text":"isequivalent(u, v; tol = Tolerance()) -> Bool\n\nWhether |u_i - v_i| ≤ ε_i for every component (see Tolerance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.isequivalent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.AbstractDominance","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractDominance\n\nSupertype of the relations accepted by covering and dominates(relation, u, v): WeakDominance and StrictDominance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.AbstractDominance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.WeakDominance","page":"API Reference","text":"WeakDominance()\n\nu weakly dominates v: it dominates or is equivalent to v. See weakly_dominates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.WeakDominance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.StrictDominance","page":"API Reference","text":"StrictDominance()\n\nu dominates v and is not equivalent to it. See dominates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.StrictDominance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.nondominated_indices","page":"API Reference","text":"nondominated_indices(F; tol = Tolerance()) -> Vector{Int}\n\nIndices, in increasing order, of the points of F that no other point of F dominates under compare. Equivalent points are all kept; use unique_points to keep one representative. The result does not depend on the order of F.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> nondominated_indices([[1.0, 3.0], [2.0, 2.0], [2.0, 4.0], [1.0, 3.0]])\n3-element Vector{Int64}:\n 1\n 2\n 4\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.nondominated_indices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.nondominated","page":"API Reference","text":"nondominated(F; tol = Tolerance())\n\nThe points of F selected by nondominated_indices, in their original order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.nondominated"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.unique_indices","page":"API Reference","text":"unique_indices(F; tol = Tolerance()) -> Vector{Int}\n\nIndices of the first point of each group of equivalent points of F (see isequivalent): a point is kept unless an earlier kept point is equivalent to it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.unique_indices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.unique_points","page":"API Reference","text":"unique_points(F; tol = Tolerance())\n\nThe points of F selected by unique_indices, in their original order.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> unique_points([[1.0, 2.0], [1.0 + 1e-12, 2.0], [0.0, 3.0]])\n2-element Vector{Vector{Float64}}:\n [1.0, 2.0]\n [0.0, 3.0]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.unique_points"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.ideal_point","page":"API Reference","text":"ideal_point(F) -> Vector\n\nComponentwise minimum of the points of F. Throws an ArgumentError if F is empty.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> ideal_point([[1.0, 3.0], [2.0, 2.0]])\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0\n 2.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.ideal_point"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.nadir_point","page":"API Reference","text":"nadir_point(F) -> Vector\n\nComponentwise maximum of the points of F. Applied to the non-dominated points of a reference set, this is the nadir point of that set. Throws an ArgumentError if F is empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.nadir_point"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/input/#Point-sets-and-tolerance","page":"Point sets and tolerance","text":"","title":"Point sets and tolerance"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/input/#Point-sets","page":"Point sets and tolerance","text":"A point set is any AbstractVector whose elements are objective vectors of the same length m ≥ 1 with finite real entries. Common forms:\n\njulia> using MultiObjectiveMetrics\n\njulia> F = [[0.0, 1.0], [0.5, 0.5], [1.0, 0.0]];       # vector of vectors\n\njulia> M = [0.0 0.5 1.0; 1.0 0.5 0.0];                 # one point per column\n\njulia> gamma_spread(eachcol(M), F) == gamma_spread(F, F)\ntrue\n\njulia> gamma_spread(eachrow(permutedims(M)), F)        # one point per row\n0.5\n\neachcol and eachrow are views: no data are copied. Every function checks that all points have the same length and finite values, and throws a DimensionMismatch or an ArgumentError otherwise. All objectives are minimized; negate an objective to maximize it. Results use the floating-point type of the input (Float32 in, Float32 out).","title":"Point sets"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/input/#Dominance-with-tolerance","page":"Point sets and tolerance","text":"Objective values produced by solvers carry rounding errors, so two runs that reach the same point rarely agree to the last bit. Comparisons therefore use a componentwise tolerance (Tolerance),\n\nvarepsilon_i = maxbigl(mathrmatol mathrmrtolcdotmax(u_i v_i)bigr)\n\nand compare classifies a pair of vectors as follows [8]:\n\nu dominates v if u_i le v_i + varepsilon_i for all i and u_j  v_j - varepsilon_j for some j;\nu and v are equivalent if u_i - v_i le varepsilon_i for all i.\n\nThe default Tolerance() takes atol = rtol = √eps(Float64) ≈ 1.5e-8, so varepsilon_i = 15cdot 10^-8max(1 u_i v_i). Tolerance(0) gives exact Pareto dominance. Every function that compares points accepts a tol keyword.\n\njulia> compare([1.0, 2.0], [1.0 + 1e-10, 2.0])\nEquivalent::DominanceRelation = 0\n\njulia> compare([1.0, 2.0], [1.0 + 1e-10, 2.0]; tol = Tolerance(0))\nDominates::DominanceRelation = 1\n\nWith a positive tolerance, dominance is not transitive: a may dominate b and b dominate c while a and c are equivalent. nondominated keeps a point exactly when no other point of the set dominates it, so the result does not depend on the order of the input.","title":"Dominance with tolerance"},{"category":"section","location":"metrics/covering/#Covering","page":"Covering","text":"The covering (C-metric) of a front B by a front A is the fraction of B weakly dominated by A [5] (Def. 9), [4]:\n\nC(A B) = frac b in B  exists a in A a preceq b B in 0 1\n\nwhere a preceq b (a covers b) means that a dominates b or equals it (Def. 4 of [5]). C(A B) = 1 means that every point of B is covered. The metric is not symmetric and C(A B) ne 1 - C(B A) in general, so report both directions.\n\nSome implementations count only strict dominance, so a point shared by both fronts is not covered. covering uses weak dominance by default and strict dominance with relation = StrictDominance():\n\njulia> using MultiObjectiveMetrics\n\njulia> A = [[0.0, 1.0], [1.0, 0.0]]; B = [[0.0, 1.0], [2.0, 0.5]];\n\njulia> covering(A, B), covering(A, B; relation = StrictDominance())\n(1.0, 0.5)","title":"Covering"},{"category":"function","location":"metrics/covering/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.covering","page":"Covering","text":"covering(A, B; relation = WeakDominance(), tol = Tolerance())\n\nCovering (C-metric) of B by A [4], [5] (Def. 9): the fraction of the points of B covered by at least one point of A,\n\nC(A, B) = |{b ∈ B : ∃ a ∈ A, a ⪰ b}| / |B|.\n\nWith the default WeakDominance, a ⪰ b means that a dominates or equals b, as in the source. StrictDominance does not count equal points; it reproduces the covering of [8] and of Metaheuristics.jl. Before counting, both sets are reduced to their non-dominated points and equivalent points are counted once.\n\nC(A, B) = 1 means that A covers all of B. The metric is not symmetric; report both C(A, B) and C(B, A). Returns NaN when B is empty.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> A = [[0.0, 1.0], [1.0, 0.0]]; B = [[0.0, 1.0], [2.0, 0.5]];\n\njulia> covering(A, B)\n1.0\n\njulia> covering(A, B; relation = StrictDominance())\n0.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.covering"},{"category":"section","location":"acknowledgements/#Acknowledgements","page":"Acknowledgements","text":"The MetricsRel.jl module of MAMBO-paper, by Nadia Fazzio, Leandro F. Prudente and María L. Schuverdt [8], motivated this package and served as its reference implementation: its handling of tolerances and edge cases guided the Conventions, and the parity values in the test suite were computed by running it (commit 37941ad). The code of MultiObjectiveMetrics.jl was written independently from the primary sources cited on each page.","title":"Acknowledgements"},{"category":"section","location":"metrics/spread/#Spread-metrics","page":"Spread metrics","text":"Γ and Δ measure how well a front of N points spreads between extreme values that are the same for all solvers [2] (Section 5.3.3). The extremes come from the reference front R, the non-dominated points of all solvers: for each objective j, f_0j = min_y in R y_j (ideal) and f_N+1j = max_y in R y_j (nadir). Let f_1j le dots le f_Nj be the sorted values of objective j in the front and delta_ij = f_i+1j - f_ij, i = 0 dots N.","title":"Spread metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"metrics/spread/#Γ-spread","page":"Spread metrics","text":"The largest gap, eq. (5.1) of [2]:\n\nGamma = max_j in 1dotsm  max_i in 0dotsN delta_ij\n\nFor two objectives this is the largest distance, in the infinity norm, between consecutive points, counting the extremes.","title":"Γ-spread"},{"category":"section","location":"metrics/spread/#Δ-spread","page":"Spread metrics","text":"The extension to m objectives, eq. (5.2) of [2], of the Δ of [3]:\n\nDelta = max_j in 1dotsm\nfracdelta_0j + delta_Nj + sum_i=1^N-1 delta_ij - bardelta_j\n     delta_0j + delta_Nj + (N-1)bardelta_j\nqquad\nbardelta_j = frac1N-1sum_i=1^N-1 delta_ij\n\nThe original Δ of [3] is defined for two objectives with Euclidean distances between consecutive points and extremes of the true front; [2] uses per-objective differences and extremes from the merged fronts, which extends it to any m.\n\nBoth are lower-is-better. Δ is zero for equally spaced points that reach both extremes. See Conventions for single points, degenerate objectives and points outside the extremes.\n\njulia> using MultiObjectiveMetrics\n\njulia> R = [[0.0, 1.0], [0.5, 0.5], [1.0, 0.0]];\n\njulia> A = [[0.0, 1.0], [0.25, 0.75], [1.0, 0.0]];\n\njulia> gamma_spread(A, R), delta_spread(A, R)\n(0.75, 0.5)","title":"Δ-spread"},{"category":"function","location":"metrics/spread/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.gamma_spread","page":"Spread metrics","text":"gamma_spread(A, R; tol = Tolerance())\ngamma_spread(A, ideal, nadir; tol = Tolerance())\n\nΓ-spread of the front A [2] (eq. 5.1): the largest gap between consecutive values of any objective, with the extreme values ideal[j] and nadir[j] added at both ends,\n\nΓ = max_j max_{i = 0,…,N} (f_{i+1,j} - f_{i,j}),\n\nwhere f_{1,j} ≤ … ≤ f_{N,j} are the sorted values of objective j in A, f_{0,j} = ideal[j] and f_{N+1,j} = nadir[j]. With a reference front R, ideal = ideal_point(R) and nadir = nadir_point(R). Lower is better.\n\nConventions (see Conventions):\n\nvalues of A outside [ideal[j], nadir[j]] are clipped to that interval;\nan objective with ideal[j] ≈ nadir[j] (under tol) is skipped;\nNaN if A is empty or every objective is skipped; Inf if A has one point.\n\nA is used as given: pass the non-dominated front of the solver. Repeated points are not removed.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> R = [[0.0, 1.0], [0.5, 0.5], [1.0, 0.0]];\n\njulia> gamma_spread(R, R)\n0.5\n\njulia> gamma_spread([[0.0, 1.0], [1.0, 0.0]], R)\n1.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.gamma_spread"},{"category":"function","location":"metrics/spread/#MultiObjectiveMetrics.delta_spread","page":"Spread metrics","text":"delta_spread(A, R; tol = Tolerance())\ndelta_spread(A, ideal, nadir; tol = Tolerance())\n\nΔ-spread of the front A [2] (eq. 5.2), the extension to m objectives of the Δ of [3]:\n\nΔ = max_j (δ_{0,j} + δ_{N,j} + Σ_{i=1}^{N-1} |δ_{i,j} - δ̄_j|) / (δ_{0,j} + δ_{N,j} + (N - 1) δ̄_j),\n\nwith δ_{i,j} = f_{i+1,j} - f_{i,j} as in gamma_spread and δ̄_j the mean of δ_{i,j}, i = 1, …, N-1. Lower is better; Δ = 0 for equally spaced points that reach both extremes.\n\nConventions (see Conventions): the same as gamma_spread. An objective in which all points of A coincide gives Δ_j = 1.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> R = [[0.0, 1.0], [0.5, 0.5], [1.0, 0.0]];\n\njulia> delta_spread(R, R)\n0.0\n\njulia> delta_spread([[0.0, 1.0], [0.25, 0.75], [1.0, 0.0]], R)\n0.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.delta_spread"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Quick-Start","page":"Quick Start","text":"Each solver returns a set of objective vectors. Reduce each set to its non-dominated points, build the reference front R from the union of all solvers, and evaluate each front against R.\n\njulia> using MultiObjectiveMetrics\n\njulia> fronts = Dict(\n           \"solver A\" => [[0.0, 1.0], [0.25, 0.75], [0.5, 0.5], [1.0, 0.0]],\n           \"solver B\" => [[0.1, 0.8], [0.6, 0.45], [0.7, 0.7]],\n       );\n\njulia> nd = Dict(name => nondominated(F) for (name, F) in fronts);\n\njulia> R = nondominated(reduce(vcat, values(nd)));\n\njulia> length(nd[\"solver B\"]), length(R)\n(2, 6)\n\njulia> purity(nd[\"solver A\"], R), purity(nd[\"solver B\"], R)\n(1.0, 1.0)\n\njulia> gamma_spread(nd[\"solver A\"], R)\n0.5\n\njulia> round(delta_spread(nd[\"solver B\"], R); digits = 2)\n0.65\n\nCovering compares two fronts directly, and the hypervolume needs an explicit reference point:\n\njulia> covering(fronts[\"solver A\"], fronts[\"solver B\"])  # no point of B is covered by A\n0.0\n\njulia> round(hypervolume(nd[\"solver A\"], [1.1, 1.1]); digits = 4)\n0.5225\n\njulia> round(normalized_hypervolume(nd[\"solver A\"], R); digits = 4)\n0.4318\n\nThe metrics compare fronts of different solvers on the same problem. To summarize many problems, see Performance profiles.","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#MultiObjectiveMetrics.jl","page":"Home","text":"Performance indicators for comparing approximations of the Pareto front produced by multiobjective optimization solvers. All objectives are minimized, and every indicator is implemented from its primary source.\n\nIndicator Function Better Source\nPurity purity higher [1, 2]\nΓ-spread gamma_spread lower [2]\nΔ-spread delta_spread lower [2, 3]\nCovering covering higher [4, 5]\nHypervolume hypervolume, normalized_hypervolume higher [4, 6]\n\nThey rest on dominance with an explicit tolerance (Tolerance, compare) and on non-dominated filtering (nondominated). The package has no runtime dependencies and accepts any vector of objective vectors, including the columns or rows of a matrix.\n\nInstallation and Quick Start: get started.\nPoint sets and tolerance: input formats and comparisons.\nPerformance profiles: comparing several solvers on many problems.\nConventions: edge cases and differences from other implementations.\nMetrics: Purity, Spread metrics, Covering, Hypervolume.\nAPI Reference, Acknowledgements and References.\n\nFor a survey of performance indicators, see [7].","title":"MultiObjectiveMetrics.jl"}]
}
